import PostFigure from '../../components/PostFigure.astro';

import usertesting2 from '../../assets/images/usertesting-2.png';
---

<PostLayout slug="measuring-experience-quality">
	<p>
		Most teams can tell you what they shipped. Far fewer can tell you whether the experience got better. Analytics show
		what people do, but not always why, or how it felt. A one-off usability test shows how things are on the day, but
		not the trend.
	</p>
	<p>
		At RAA I set up a UX benchmarking practice to fill that gap. It's one of the pieces of work I'm most proud of,
		because it changed how decisions got made.
	</p>

	<h2>Start by defining success</h2>
	<p>
		Before measuring anything, we agree what success looks like for each product, with the product manager and the
		team. That's the same question I ask at every kickoff: how will we know it worked, and what's the number today?
		Benchmarking makes that question answerable for the experience itself, not just for sales or clicks.
	</p>

	<h2>Four measures, the same way every time</h2>
	<p>Each benchmark measures four things:</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Effectiveness:</strong> can people complete the task at all?</li>
		<li><strong>Efficiency:</strong> how long does it take, and how many errors are there?</li>
		<li><strong>Satisfaction:</strong> how do people rate the experience?</li>
		<li><strong>Desirability:</strong> does it feel the way the brand wants it to feel?</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Every study follows the same protocol, so the results can be compared over time. That's the point: a change in
		score only means something when the method doesn't change. On one home and contents insurance quote benchmark, 39
		people completed the task, 1 didn't, and the average time was 4 minutes. On its own that's a snapshot. Repeated
		after each release, it becomes a trend.
	</p>

	<h2>Built on research operations</h2>
	<p>
		Benchmarking needed foundations that didn't exist yet. Research findings used to be produced for one project and
		then lost. I set up research operations from scratch: a repository that tagged findings to products and journey
		stages, a standard testing process, and a regular benchmarking cadence.
	</p>
	<PostFigure
		src={usertesting2}
		alt="Moderated usability session showing a participant's screen with a tagged transcript and observation notes"
		caption="A moderated session with a tagged transcript and observation notes."
	/>

	<h2>Closing the loop</h2>
	<p>
		Benchmark results feed straight into the journey map, at the stage where each study ran. When a measure drops, the
		map shows which stage is affected and what's already planned to fix it. When a release improves the score, there's
		evidence the change worked. When it doesn't, that's a signal to go back and understand why.
	</p>
	<p>
		Without that connection, benchmarking produces reports that get filed. With it, the numbers shape what the team
		works on next.
	</p>
</PostLayout>
